============================================================
1. REPOSITORY STRUCTURE
============================================================

Use a multi-repo architecture:

github.com/koc-council/
  ├── frontend        (React SPA)
  ├── backend         (FastAPI + Celery)
  ├── infra           (K3s manifests + Helm charts)
  ├── docs            (Architecture, ERD, API spec, RBAC)
  └── ops             (CI/CD workflows, scripts)

Reason:
- Clear separation of concerns
- Independent versioning
- Independent deployments
- Easier onboarding


============================================================
2. BRANCHING STRATEGY
============================================================

Use GitHub Flow with environment branches:

main            → production
staging         → staging environment
dev             → shared development

Feature branches:
feature/<name>  → new features
bugfix/<name>   → fixes
hotfix/<name>   → urgent production fixes

Rules:
- Only staging merges into main
- Only dev merges into staging
- Feature branches merge into dev


============================================================
3. PULL REQUEST WORKFLOW
============================================================

Every change must go through a PR.

PR Requirements:
- Clear description
- Screenshots for UI changes
- Linked issue number
- Passing CI checks
- At least one reviewer approval
- No failing tests
- No merge conflicts

Labels:
- enhancement
- bug
- documentation
- infra
- security
- breaking-change


============================================================
4. ISSUE TRACKING & PROJECT BOARDS
============================================================

Use GitHub Projects for Kanban-style tracking:

Columns:
- Backlog
- Ready
- In Progress
- In Review
- Testing
- Done

Issue Types:
- Feature request
- Bug report
- Task
- Epic (multi-feature)
- Spike (research)

Every issue must include:
- Description
- Acceptance criteria
- Priority
- Assigned owner
- Estimated effort


============================================================
5. VERSIONING & RELEASES
============================================================

Use semantic versioning:

MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH

Examples:
1.0.0 → first production release  
1.1.0 → new features  
1.1.1 → bug fixes  

Release Process:
1. Merge staging → main
2. Tag release (v1.0.0)
3. GitHub Actions builds release artifacts
4. Helm charts updated with new image tags
5. Production deployment triggered


============================================================
6. CI/CD WITH GITHUB ACTIONS
============================================================

Workflows:
- frontend-ci.yml
- backend-ci.yml
- infra-ci.yml
- deploy-staging.yml
- deploy-production.yml

CI Tasks:
- Install dependencies
- Run linters (ESLint, Black, Ruff)
- Run tests (frontend + backend)
- Build Docker images
- Push images to registry
- Validate Helm charts

CD Tasks:
- Deploy to staging on merge to staging
- Deploy to production on tagged release


============================================================
7. CODE REVIEWS & QUALITY RULES
============================================================

Review Checklist:
- Code follows standards
- No secrets committed
- No console.log or debug prints
- Tests included for new logic
- API changes documented
- RBAC rules enforced
- No large PRs (>500 lines) without discussion

Review Roles:
- Frontend devs review frontend PRs
- Backend devs review backend PRs
- Infra devs review Helm/K3s PRs
- Lead dev reviews all critical PRs


============================================================
8. SECURITY PRACTICES
============================================================

Rules:
- No secrets in repos
- Use GitHub Secret Scanning
- Use Dependabot for dependency updates
- Use branch protection rules
- Require signed commits (optional)
- Require PR reviews for main/staging

Protected Branches:
- main
- staging

Allowed Actions:
- Only approved GitHub Actions
- No third-party actions without review


============================================================
9. DOCUMENTATION MANAGEMENT
============================================================

docs repo contains:
- System architecture overview
- ERD
- API specification
- RBAC matrix
- Coding standards
- Deployment architecture
- Logging & monitoring strategy
- Celery task architecture

Rules:
- Every major feature requires documentation
- Docs updated before merging PR
- Architecture diagrams stored in /docs/diagrams


============================================================
10. COLLABORATION & COMMUNICATION
============================================================

Use:
- GitHub Discussions for design debates
- GitHub Issues for tasks
- GitHub Projects for planning
- GitHub Wiki for onboarding guides

Weekly Workflow:
- Monday: Sprint planning
- Daily: Standup updates via GitHub comments
- Friday: Review PRs + merge to dev


============================================================
11. RELEASE & DEPLOYMENT PIPELINE
============================================================

dev → staging → production

Staging Deployment:
- Automatic on merge to staging
- Used for QA, council review

Production Deployment:
- Manual approval required
- Triggered by release tag
- Helm upgrade executed via GitHub Actions


============================================================
12. BACKUP & RECOVERY
============================================================

Store:
- Helm chart versions
- K3s manifests
- Database migration scripts
- Release artifacts

Recovery Steps:
1. Checkout last stable release
2. Helm rollback
3. Restore DB backup
4. Redeploy workers
